WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
In chapter one, a stretch of the protein's own chain worked as an address.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.860
How do you know a protein really went inside?

3
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.980
And how do you prove which stretch sent it?

4
00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:16.063
Start with the classic test.

5
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:22.763
Make the protein in a test tube that also holds microsomes, sealed vesicles of ER

6
00:00:22.763 --> 00:00:28.403
membrane. Then add a protease, an enzyme that chews up exposed protein.

7
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:33.623
The added protease cannot cross an intact microsome membrane.

8
00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:37.223
A chain outside the vesicles is chewed up.

9
00:00:38.403 --> 00:00:41.943
A chain inside is shielded, so its band survives on the gel.

10
00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:45.937
But maybe that protein simply resists protease.

11
00:00:47.077 --> 00:00:49.577
So add a detergent, which dissolves membranes.

12
00:00:50.797 --> 00:00:54.037
If the band now vanishes, the membrane was the shield.

13
00:00:55.167 --> 00:01:01.927
In 1975, Blobel and Dobberstein tested an antibody light chain, a secreted protein.

14
00:01:03.447 --> 00:01:06.587
Globin, which stays in the cytosol, was made the same way.

15
00:01:07.747 --> 00:01:09.227
Which survives the protease?

16
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:17.217
The light chain came out trimmed and protected.

17
00:01:18.457 --> 00:01:22.237
That happened only when the vesicles were there while it was being made.

18
00:01:23.737 --> 00:01:24.817
Globin was not protected.

19
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:31.580
The parts came from three species: rabbit ribosomes, dog membranes, and mouse

20
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:35.340
messenger RNA. They still worked together.

21
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.400
That ruled out a special secretory ribosome and supported an address in the growing

22
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.400
protein.

23
00:01:42.983 --> 00:01:44.483
So here is the idea.

24
00:01:45.743 --> 00:01:47.863
A sorting claim needs two kinds of test.

25
00:01:49.163 --> 00:01:52.503
One shows the protein got in; the other shows what sent it.

26
00:01:53.383 --> 00:01:55.543
To find the address, there are two tests.

27
00:01:56.763 --> 00:01:59.923
Move a stretch onto a protein that stays in the cytosol.

28
00:02:01.043 --> 00:02:04.043
If that passenger moves too, the stretch is sufficient.

29
00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:07.443
Or delete the stretch from its own protein.

30
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:12.703
If the protein is stranded in the cytosol, the stretch is necessary.

31
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:16.683
Chapter 1's mouse enzyme was the first kind.

32
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:25.403
Yeast cytochrome oxidase IV precursor's first 53 amino acids carried mouse DHFR into

33
00:02:25.403 --> 00:02:32.243
the matrix. Adding SKL to a bacterial enzyme's carboxyl end sent it into

34
00:02:32.243 --> 00:02:33.903
mammalian-cell peroxisomes.

35
00:02:34.983 --> 00:02:36.463
Now the other test.

36
00:02:37.903 --> 00:02:43.643
Deleting luciferase's carboxyl tail, no more than 12 amino acids long, left it in

37
00:02:43.643 --> 00:02:47.423
the cytosol. A signal in that tail is necessary.

38
00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:55.007
Two cautions. Working for one passenger does not prove it works for every passenger.

39
00:02:56.387 --> 00:03:03.127
And SKL is picky. In luciferase, only a few swaps to similar amino acids kept it

40
00:03:03.127 --> 00:03:04.127
working.

41
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:09.390
And when a protein carries two signals, only an experiment can say where it goes.

42
00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.450
Protection shows it got in; moving and deleting show what sent it.
